{"step_index":2265,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:14:44Z","input_tokens":2188,"cache_read_tokens":33569,"output_tokens":14392,"thinking":"Determining the CSS styles required for the Phase 5 Learning Workspace involves specifying rules for the container and top bar elements. The workspace container will use a maximum width or full width, a light background color, and padding. The top bar will be a flexible container with content space-between, centered items and potentially a sticky or top margin.\n\n\nNow focusing on the specific styling for the workspace sections. The problem header will need a white background, subtle border, rounded corners, padding, and shadow. A two-column layout will be implemented using a grid with responsive behavior and distinct styling for each column.\n\n\nFocusing on the tab styling, it appears necessary to set appropriate hover and active states using specific background and border colors, along with transition effects for visual feedback. The pane body requires padding, scroll handling, and maximum/minimum heights that need to be carefully calculated. Finally, teaching content will have specific margin, font, and layout characteristics, including a distinct structure for headers, prose, and example cards.\n\n\nSpecific style rules for example elements include background colors, font sizes, margins, and border-radius properties. Callout boxes use a distinct background and border to highlight information. The \"Pattern\" tab requires styling for the hero card, badge, title, description, and a grid layout for subcards. The subcards themselves need background, border, padding, and heading styles, alongside a styled steps list.\n\n\nStyling the step list requires absolute positioning of the counter, ensuring proper indentation. The walkthrough box needs a distinct background, border, and padding.\n\nThe editor toolbar demands a flexible layout with specific styling for badges, autosave